  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe will use a combination of gradient descent and coordinate-wise optimization to improve the existing approach. The central idea is to transform the non-convex optimization problem into a form that allows efficient local search from multiple starting points. We introduce a smoothing technique using a piecewise linear approximation to handle the non-differentiable points, and apply a multi-start optimization with adaptive step sizing to navigate the complex landscape. The method ensures we stay within the integral constraint while minimizing the maximum overlap.\n\nBottleneck addressed: The original method using random starting points and local search was inefficient and prone to local optima. Our approach using gradient-based with coordinate smoothing will allow for faster convergence and better exploitation of promising regions.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Representation: n_points = 280 samples on [0,2]\n- Variables: h(x) \u2208 [0,1], \u2211h(x) = 140\n- Objective: C5 = max_k \u222b|h(x)(1-h(x+k))| dx  (mode='full' correlation)\n- Minimization: Reduce C5 by optimizing h(x)\n- Formulation:\n  - Smoothed objective: Piecewise linear approximation\n  - Integral constraint: \u2211h(x) = 140\n  - Domain constraints: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n1. Initialize using supplied parent if available\n2. Generate 16 initial candidates:\n   - 1 from parent\n   - 15 from modified parent with noise variations\n   - Normalize to integral = 140\n3. For each candidate:\n   a. Apply gradient-based optimization\n   b. Use coordinate-wise updates with adaptive step sizes\n   c. Handle maximum overlap via Lagrange multiplier method\n   d. Maintain integral constraint using projection\n   e. Clip values to [0,1] after each update\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - Step adjustment: Dynamic step sizing based on local gradient\n   - Dimension reduction: 1D line search along coordinate directions\n   - Candidate acceptance: Only accept improvements or random simplex\n   - Target tracking: Monitor progress against previous records\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   - Time estimate: 60s for grid evaluation, 30s per optimization\n   - Early termination: If improvement stalls after 1000 function evaluations\n   - Maximum evaluations: 1000 per candidate (18 per second)\n   - Integral adjustment: Projected gradient method\n   - Fallback: best_candidate from initial evaluations\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Feasibility checks: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h[i] \u2248 140\n- Objective calculation: np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n- Maximum constraint: dx * np.max(corr)\n- Binary values: Use rectangular pulses for discrete steps\n- Output validation: Re-evaluate with np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n- Integral maintenance: After each update, re-normalize to 140",
